1
00:00:01,550 --> 00:00:03,590
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:04,270
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:05,570
My name is Catherine.

4
00:00:06,220 --> 00:00:10,190
And today we're doing something that makes a lot of people very nervous when they come to America.

5
00:00:10,820 --> 00:00:11,820
That's right.

6
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:18,030
Today we are arriving to the airport, but before we can leave, we need to pass through customs and immigration.

7
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:18,850
All right.

8
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:19,890
Customs and immigration.

9
00:00:20,290 --> 00:00:26,150
So anyone who comes into the United States, American, not American, doesn't matter, you have to go through this gate.

10
00:00:26,390 --> 00:00:29,870
So let's learn about some of these phrases and questions in today's dialogue.

11
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:38,020
Good afternoon.

12
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:39,840
Passport and arrival card, please.

13
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:40,800
Here you are.

14
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:42,040
Where are you coming from?

15
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:42,440
China.

16
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:44,540
Is this your country of birth or residence?

17
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:46,680
I just worked there.

18
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:48,540
What is the purpose of your visit to the United States?

19
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:49,540
I'm here on vacation.

20
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:52,440
How long do you plan to stay in the United States?

21
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:53,820
Almost three weeks.

22
00:00:54,460 --> 00:00:58,480
Sir, you didn't fill out the information on your arrival card of where you'll be staying.

23
00:00:59,190 --> 00:01:04,530
Oh, I'm sorry, but there are a couple of different places I will travel to within the United States, so I wasn't sure what to put.

24
00:01:04,730 --> 00:01:08,470
You must specify an address on the place where you will spend most of your time.

25
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:12,760
Okay.

26
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:13,070
Here you are.

27
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:16,210
Do you have enough means to support yourself while you're here?

28
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:18,730
Yes, I have some travelers checks and two credit cards.

29
00:01:19,170 --> 00:01:19,610
Very good.

30
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:21,170
Do you have anything to declare?

31
00:01:21,550 --> 00:01:21,790
Nope.

32
00:01:22,230 --> 00:01:23,570
I only have my clothes and camera.

33
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:24,670
Very well, sir.

34
00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:26,250
Welcome to the United States.

35
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:27,450
Enjoy your visit.

36
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:33,200
All right, we're back.

37
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:35,530
So now let's take a look at some of that language takeaway.

38
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:39,600
Language takeaway.

39
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:46,100
The first thing we have to learn about is the arrival card.

40
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:50,120
So good afternoon, passport and arrival card, please.

41
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:51,420
That's the first line of the dialogue.

42
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:52,240
That's right.

43
00:01:53,080 --> 00:02:06,440
So the arrival card, usually you will get on the airplane before you land, and basically you just have to put your name, your passport number, where you're from, and basic information like this that you must give to the immigration officer.

44
00:02:06,590 --> 00:02:06,790
Great.

45
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:09,010
So what is the opposite of arrival card?

46
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:12,240
What do you fill out when you leave America?

47
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:13,240
That's right.

48
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:16,000
Some countries require you to fill out a departure card.

49
00:02:16,190 --> 00:02:16,390
All right.

50
00:02:16,570 --> 00:02:22,970
So some countries, not all, but you'll usually get an arrival card, which means that you have to give your name and basic information.

51
00:02:23,290 --> 00:02:23,750
That's right.

52
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:31,640
So we are talking to the immigration officer, and he asks, is this your country of birth or residence?

53
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:33,960
All right, let's break that down.

54
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:34,860
Country of birth.

55
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:40,900
So birth, birthday, that makes me think of where I was born.

56
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:43,260
So what country were you born in?

57
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:44,960
That's another way to ask this question.

58
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:45,600
That's right.

59
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:57,880
So since we said we are coming from China, the immigration officer wants to know if we are Chinese or if we simply live there, and that's why he says or residence.

60
00:02:57,880 --> 00:02:59,900
So residence, it's a noun, it's a thing.

61
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,640
My country of residence is the place where I live.

62
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:09,240
So I'm American, but maybe my country of residence is France because I live in France.

63
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:10,040
That's right.

64
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:13,420
So it's different from your country of birth.

65
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:22,510
Now moving on, when the immigration officer was asking us about money, we said that we have some traveler's checks and two credit cards.

66
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:30,180
All right, so credit cards are pretty clear, but what exactly are traveler's checks and how are they different from normal checks?

67
00:03:30,180 --> 00:03:38,690
So traveler's checks are kind of a convenient way to carry money because you have these little pieces of paper that have a certain amount on them.

68
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:48,730
So for example, $500 each, and if you lose it, it's not a problem because no one can exchange those checks for cash unless it's you.

69
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:52,190
All right, so my traveler's check, I buy these from the bank.

70
00:03:52,880 --> 00:04:04,510
They have my name on them, my passport number on them, and to spend them as money, I need to show my passport or proof of identification or proof of identity.

71
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:13,920
So basically, if someone steals my traveler's checks, if I lose my traveler's checks, I don't have to worry because no one else can spend them but me.

72
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:14,920
Exactly.

73
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:18,830
They were very, very popular back before credit cards were so popular.

74
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:22,680
Now you don't really see too many traveler's checks, but it's still convenient.

75
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:24,880
All right, so a lot of language there.

76
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:26,490
Why don't we listen to our dialogue again?

77
00:04:34,030 --> 00:04:34,470
Good afternoon.

78
00:04:35,090 --> 00:04:36,290
Passport and arrival card, please.

79
00:04:36,590 --> 00:04:37,250
Here you are.

80
00:04:37,630 --> 00:04:38,490
Where are you coming from?

81
00:04:38,710 --> 00:04:38,910
China.

82
00:04:39,550 --> 00:04:40,970
Is this your country of birth or residence?

83
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:43,080
I just worked there.

84
00:04:43,080 --> 00:04:44,990
What is the purpose of your visit to the United States?

85
00:04:45,290 --> 00:04:46,010
I'm here on vacation.

86
00:04:46,690 --> 00:04:48,890
How long do you plan to stay in the United States?

87
00:04:49,290 --> 00:04:50,270
Almost three weeks.

88
00:04:50,930 --> 00:04:54,930
Sir, you didn't fill out the information on your arrival card of where you'll be staying.

89
00:04:55,640 --> 00:05:00,980
Oh, I'm sorry, but there are a couple of different places I will travel to within the United States, so I wasn't sure what to put.

90
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:04,920
You must specify an address on the place where you will spend most of your time.

91
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:09,200
Okay.

92
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:09,510
Here you are.

93
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,670
Do you have enough means to support yourself while you're here?

94
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:15,190
Yes, I have some traveler's checks and two credit cards.

95
00:05:15,610 --> 00:05:16,070
Very good.

96
00:05:16,610 --> 00:05:17,610
Do you have anything to declare?

97
00:05:18,010 --> 00:05:18,250
Nope.

98
00:05:18,670 --> 00:05:20,010
I only have my clothes and camera.

99
00:05:20,430 --> 00:05:21,130
Very well, sir.

100
00:05:21,530 --> 00:05:22,690
Welcome to the United States.

101
00:05:23,310 --> 00:05:23,910
Enjoy your visit.

102
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:29,160
All right, we're back.

103
00:05:29,160 --> 00:05:34,620
So now let's take a look at some of those key questions that the immigration officer asks us on Fluency Builder.

104
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:37,420
Fluency Builder.

105
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:45,680
All right, this first sentence, this first question is actually something that a lot of learners of English have a hard time with.

106
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:46,870
Where are you coming from?

107
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:48,480
Right.

108
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:52,210
So the immigration officer is sitting there at his desk and you are arriving on an airplane.

109
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:56,990
So what he wants to know is what country are you arriving from?

110
00:05:57,690 --> 00:06:05,100
So if I am on a flight from France to America, I say I'm arriving from France.

111
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:07,220
I'm coming from France.

112
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:07,800
That's right.

113
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:09,120
But here's a big thing.

114
00:06:09,660 --> 00:06:12,060
What if he says, where are you from?

115
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:14,440
Now he's asking you for your nationality.

116
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:17,520
So then you wouldn't say France because you're not French.

117
00:06:17,900 --> 00:06:18,500
I'm not French.

118
00:06:18,660 --> 00:06:19,000
I'm American.

119
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:23,340
So I'd say I am American, but I'm coming from France.

120
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:36,100
So this is a very typical question that English learners confuse because as you can see, it's very, very similar, but you just have one word that you add on and it completely changes the question.

121
00:06:36,460 --> 00:06:40,240
So when the immigration officer asks you, where are you coming from?

122
00:06:40,820 --> 00:06:43,840
You tell them what country you got on the plane.

123
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:44,960
All right.

124
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:48,040
So where did you leave to come to America?

125
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:51,280
Did you leave from France, China, Ghana?

126
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:52,780
So that's the answer to that question.

127
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:54,080
Exactly.

128
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:58,840
Most of the time, immigration officers will never ask you where you are from because they will see your passport.

129
00:06:59,200 --> 00:06:59,460
They know.

130
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:00,020
They know.

131
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:01,560
So, okay.

132
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:06,500
Now, once we give them our passport, we tell them that we just work in China.

133
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:07,420
We're not Chinese.

134
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:12,260
He asks, what is the purpose of your visit to the United States?

135
00:07:12,750 --> 00:07:14,890
What's the purpose of your visit?

136
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:17,350
Now purpose means goal or aim.

137
00:07:17,870 --> 00:07:24,770
So there are a couple of standard answers to this question, but basically the immigration officer wants to know, why are you in America?

138
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:26,880
Why are you coming here?

139
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:27,070
That's right.

140
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:31,650
So you have, as Catherine says, a couple of different ways of answering that.

141
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:34,210
We're saying here that we are here on vacation.

142
00:07:34,570 --> 00:07:35,090
I'm here on vacation.

143
00:07:35,710 --> 00:07:35,950
Tourism.

144
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:37,520
Tourism.

145
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:38,520
Business.

146
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:39,520
Right.

147
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:40,520
Seeing family.

148
00:07:40,520 --> 00:07:41,410
These are all acceptable answers.

149
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:42,270
That's right.

150
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:51,830
So now that we've told the officer all of this information, we actually forgot to fill out our address on our arrival card.

151
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:55,490
So he says that we must specify an address.

152
00:07:56,050 --> 00:07:56,330
All right.

153
00:07:56,720 --> 00:07:58,530
So to specify, this is a verb.

154
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:06,860
It means to indicate or write down or identify because right now there is no address where we're going to stay.

155
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:12,480
So to specify means to basically to tell someone where it is, to be exact.

156
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:13,580
That's right.

157
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:19,780
So you need to specify an address of where you will be during the time you're in the United States.

158
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:21,360
It could be a hotel, any place.

159
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:22,360
Right.

160
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:23,040
But it can't be like Chicago.

161
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:23,580
Right.

162
00:08:23,980 --> 00:08:24,760
No, it can't be a city.

163
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:25,820
It's got to be an address.

164
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:26,280
Address.

165
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:30,440
So 10 South Street, Chicago, Illinois.

166
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:30,630
That's right.

167
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:39,370
Now we told the immigration officer where we're going to be and he asks a very important question, also very, very common.

168
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:44,030
Do you have enough means to support yourself while you are here?

169
00:08:44,590 --> 00:08:48,110
So means in this instance means resources or money.

170
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:53,240
So do you have enough money to pay for your housing, your food?

171
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:53,690
That's the question.

172
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:55,240
Right.

173
00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:55,870
So means to support yourself.

174
00:08:56,740 --> 00:08:58,480
That means can you pay for yourself?

175
00:08:58,960 --> 00:08:59,760
Who's paying for this trip?

176
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:00,240
Exactly.

177
00:09:01,440 --> 00:09:05,640
So this is why we say, yeah, we have some traveler's checks and a couple of credit cards.

178
00:09:05,640 --> 00:09:09,870
And lastly, we also need to answer if we have anything to declare.

179
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:11,130
But what does this mean?

180
00:09:11,320 --> 00:09:12,600
To declare what?

181
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:13,670
To declare at customs.

182
00:09:14,210 --> 00:09:14,410
Okay.

183
00:09:14,470 --> 00:09:16,490
But we don't normally ask to declare at customs.

184
00:09:16,750 --> 00:09:18,610
We just say, do you have anything to declare?

185
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:25,640
This is because normally when we travel to other countries, there are special taxes for certain goods.

186
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:34,880
For example, if I want to come to America and I have three bottles of wine, I have to give one away because I'm only allowed to bring in two bottles.

187
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:35,880
Right.

188
00:09:35,880 --> 00:09:38,390
So I have to declare two bottles of wine or money.

189
00:09:38,920 --> 00:09:41,280
I'm only allowed to bring $10,000.

190
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:46,230
If I bring $15,000, I have to write that down and say, I have this extra money and then I pay tax.

191
00:09:47,440 --> 00:09:50,430
So basically they will ask you, what are you bringing to America?

192
00:09:51,080 --> 00:09:59,080
And if there are special things like money or things you will sell or alcohol, cigarettes, you have to declare these.

193
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:01,000
You have to explain what you're carrying.

194
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:01,210
That's right.

195
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:03,030
So this is a very important question.

196
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:15,250
Also most of the time, obviously if you're on tourism, you don't have much to declare, but maybe like if Catherine says, maybe you are going to buy a house in the United States and you have $20,000 in your luggage, then you need to tell them that.

197
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:15,890
That's right.

198
00:10:16,230 --> 00:10:19,170
So that's called declaring, to declare at customs.

199
00:10:19,730 --> 00:10:19,970
All right.

200
00:10:20,090 --> 00:10:23,150
So let's take a break and listen to our dialogue one last time.

201
00:10:30,570 --> 00:10:31,210
Good afternoon.

202
00:10:31,830 --> 00:10:33,010
Passport and arrival card, please.

203
00:10:33,310 --> 00:10:33,970
Here you are.

204
00:10:34,390 --> 00:10:35,210
Where are you coming from?

205
00:10:35,450 --> 00:10:35,630
China.

206
00:10:36,250 --> 00:10:37,710
Is this your country of birth or residence?

207
00:10:38,170 --> 00:10:38,950
I just worked there.

208
00:10:39,330 --> 00:10:41,710
What is the purpose of your visit to the United States?

209
00:10:42,030 --> 00:10:42,690
I'm here on vacation.

210
00:10:43,410 --> 00:10:45,630
How long do you plan to stay in the United States?

211
00:10:45,990 --> 00:10:47,050
Almost three weeks.

212
00:10:47,630 --> 00:10:51,630
Sir, you didn't fill out the information on your arrival card of where you'll be staying.

213
00:10:52,370 --> 00:10:57,690
Oh, I'm sorry, but there are a couple of different places I will travel to within the United States, so I wasn't sure what to put.

214
00:10:57,890 --> 00:11:01,650
You must specify an address on the place where you will spend most of your time.

215
00:11:03,400 --> 00:11:05,920
Okay.

216
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:06,230
Here you are.

217
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:09,390
Do you have enough means to support yourself while you're here?

218
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:11,890
Yes, I have some traveler's checks and two credit cards.

219
00:11:12,330 --> 00:11:12,790
Very good.

220
00:11:13,290 --> 00:11:14,350
Do you have anything to declare?

221
00:11:14,690 --> 00:11:14,970
Nope.

222
00:11:15,370 --> 00:11:16,730
I only have my clothes and camera.

223
00:11:17,130 --> 00:11:17,830
Very well, sir.

224
00:11:18,230 --> 00:11:19,410
Welcome to the United States.

225
00:11:20,030 --> 00:11:20,610
Enjoy your visit.

226
00:11:24,870 --> 00:11:25,390
All right.

227
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:30,710
So when you're arriving to immigration, obviously all these questions are very, very common.

228
00:11:30,930 --> 00:11:31,790
You will be asked.

229
00:11:33,030 --> 00:11:37,670
But when you're arriving there, there are actually two different sections that you have to go to, right?

230
00:11:37,990 --> 00:11:38,210
That's right.

231
00:11:38,330 --> 00:11:39,330
There's two different lines.

232
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,150
Maybe many lines, but they'll have a sign on top.

233
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:52,720
One says US passports or US passport holders, and that means the line for Americans coming home to America.

234
00:11:52,720 --> 00:11:57,520
But for most people who are traveling to America, they're foreigners, they have to go to the other line.

235
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:00,560
So that's non-US passport holders.

236
00:12:01,080 --> 00:12:01,260
That's right.

237
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:06,720
Now remember that you don't really have to get nervous when you're talking to the immigration officer.

238
00:12:08,650 --> 00:12:11,130
You have to take it easy and just try to answer the questions.

239
00:12:11,680 --> 00:12:22,200
Most of them will speak a couple of words in many popular languages like Chinese or Spanish or Arabic.

240
00:12:22,200 --> 00:12:27,280
Because obviously some people that go on holiday to the United States, maybe they don't speak English.

241
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:27,430
That's right.

242
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:28,830
So there's nothing to be worried about.

243
00:12:29,280 --> 00:12:31,890
These people are there to make sure that America is safe.

244
00:12:32,180 --> 00:12:33,310
They want to check the bags.

245
00:12:33,350 --> 00:12:35,730
They want to know who's coming into the country.

246
00:12:36,350 --> 00:12:40,190
And they're just doing their job being basically policing the borders.

247
00:12:41,610 --> 00:12:42,470
But it's pretty simple.

248
00:12:42,470 --> 00:12:45,770
The most annoying part of customs for me is waiting in line.

249
00:12:46,010 --> 00:12:50,670
